From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] esp32: USB networking (NCM) compiled in, turned on from code
 through machine.USBDevice.

network.USBD_NCM drove lwIP's raw API, which ESP-IDF allows only from its
own tcpip task, so the esp32 port gets a driver of its own: an esp_netif
with a custom I/O driver, a link-local address and ESP-IDF's DHCP server
for the computer, and no gateway. Frames the stack sends are queued to the
scheduler, where TinyUSB runs, and wait there while its transmit blocks are
full; received ones are copied, since the stack holds on to a buffer past
the callback, and go to esp_netif_receive(). Blocks are 8 KB on esp32, so
one pass of the scheduler moves several frames.

NCM was a build-time choice that put it in the built-in descriptor beside
the REPL, where it took two of an S3's four IN endpoints for good. Now
MICROPY_HW_USB_NCM_BUILTIN says whether it is built in (as before, the
default elsewhere), and esp32 sets it to 0 and compiles NCM in wherever the
runtime USB device and the network stack are. Code turns it on by giving
machine.USBDevice a CDC-NCM function's descriptors (usb-device-net): the
runtime driver leaves that function to TinyUSB's NCM driver, which moves
its frames in C, and creating network.USBD_NCM() hands TinyUSB its transfer
blocks, so NCM costs no endpoints and no RAM until code chooses it.

machine.USBDevice.EP_IN_MAX says how many IN endpoints the controller has
besides endpoint 0, for usb.device to check a set of functions against.
The shared pieces learn that ESP-IDF's lwipopts.h has no
LWIP_MDNS_RESPONDER and that the built-in MAC string is upper-case hex, as
Windows requires.
---
